<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<section xml:id="one-way-anova">
  <title>One-Way ANOVA and Tukey Comparisons</title>
  <objectives>
    <ol marker="1)">
      <li>
        <p>State the hypotheses and requirements for one-way ANOVA.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Complete missing entries in an ANOVA table and interpret R output.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Use Tukey comparisons to identify and describe supported pairwise differences.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </objectives>
  <subsection xml:id="av-introduction">
    <title>Comparing Several Population Means</title>
    <definition xml:id="av-definition">
      <title>One-Way Analysis of Variance</title>
      <statement>
        <p><term>One-way analysis of variance</term>, or <term>one-way ANOVA</term>, compares population means for groups defined by one categorical explanatory variable. The response variable is quantitative. The categorical variable is the <term>factor</term>, and its categories are the <term>levels</term>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>Let <m>k</m> be the number of groups, <m>n_i</m> the sample size in Group <m>i</m>, and <m>N=n_1+\cdots+n_k</m> the total sample size. The group population means are <m>\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_k</m>.</p>
    <assemblage xml:id="av-hypotheses">
      <title>Hypotheses for One-Way ANOVA</title>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>H_0:\mu_1=\mu_2=\cdots=\mu_k</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>H_a:\text{At least one population mean differs.}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>The alternative does not say that every mean differs from every other mean. Rejecting the null establishes evidence of some difference; follow-up comparisons identify which differences are supported.</p>
    </assemblage>
    <assemblage xml:id="av-conditions">
      <title>Requirements for Classical One-Way ANOVA</title>
      <p>
        <ol marker="1)">
          <li>
            <p>One quantitative response is measured on independent experimental or sampling units. Groups are formed by one factor, and data come from an appropriate random sample or randomized experiment.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The populations within groups, or equivalently the model errors, are approximately normal. Inspect residual plots and watch for influential outliers, especially with small groups.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The population variances are approximately equal across groups. Similar sample sizes improve robustness, but do not excuse severe variance differences or outliers.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>Repeated measurements on the same subject cannot be treated as independent observations. For the health-study example, use one reduction per participant.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </assemblage>
    <p>All studies and small data sets in these notes are hypothetical classroom examples. The larger health-study file is simulated and contains no patient records.</p>
    <p>Keep full precision in intermediate calculations. Round probabilities to four decimal places and final measurements to a sensible precision for their units.</p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="av-variation-table">
    <title>Variation and the ANOVA Table</title>
    <definition xml:id="av-variation">
      <title>Between-Group and Within-Group Variation</title>
      <statement>
        <p><term>Between-group variation</term> describes how far the group sample means are from the overall mean. <term>Within-group variation</term> describes how far individual observations are from their own group mean. ANOVA compares these sources of variation.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>The <term>grand mean</term> is the mean of all observations, <m>\bar x_{\mathrm{grand}}=\dfrac{\sum_{i=1}^k n_i\bar x_i}{N}</m>. A <term>sum of squares</term> (SS) measures variation. A <term>mean square</term> (MS) is a sum of squares divided by its degrees of freedom.</p>
    <assemblage xml:id="av-calculations">
      <title>Relationships Used to Complete an ANOVA Table</title>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>SS_{\mathrm{total}}=SS_{\mathrm{between}}+SS_{\mathrm{within}}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>df_{\mathrm{between}}=k-1,\qquad df_{\mathrm{within}}=N-k,\qquad df_{\mathrm{total}}=N-1</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>MS_{\mathrm{between}}=\dfrac{SS_{\mathrm{between}}}{k-1},\qquad MS_{\mathrm{within}}=\dfrac{SS_{\mathrm{within}}}{N-k}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>F=\dfrac{MS_{\mathrm{between}}}{MS_{\mathrm{within}}}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Under the null hypothesis and the model requirements, compare the statistic with <m>F_{k-1,N-k}</m>. The p-value is the right-tail area.</p>
    </assemblage>
    <table xml:id="av-table-template">
      <title>One-Way ANOVA Table</title>
      <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Source</cell>
          <cell>df</cell>
          <cell>SS</cell>
          <cell>MS</cell>
          <cell>F</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Between groups</cell>
          <cell>
            <m>k-1</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SS_B</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SS_B/(k-1)</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>MS_B/MS_W</m>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <line>Within groups</line>
            <line>(error)</line>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>N-k</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SS_W</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SS_W/(N-k)</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>—</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Total</cell>
          <cell>
            <m>N-1</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SS_B+SS_W</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>—</cell>
          <cell>—</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>A larger F-statistic means the group means are more separated relative to the variation within groups. R labels the within-group row <c>Residuals</c> and usually omits the Total row. Empty F and MS entries in the Total row are not missing calculations.</p>
    <program language="r">
# Use this after finding the statistic and degrees of freedom.
pf(F_stat, df1 = k - 1, df2 = N - k,
   lower.tail = FALSE)
</program>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="av-r-workflow">
    <title>One-Way ANOVA in R</title>
    <p>Put the response in one numeric column and the group labels in a factor column. The formula <c>response ~ group</c> tells R which variable is the response and which variable defines the groups.</p>
    <program language="r">
dat$group &lt;- factor(dat$group)
boxplot(response ~ group, data = dat,
        xlab = "Group", ylab = "Response")
fit &lt;- aov(response ~ group, data = dat)
summary(fit)

# Check normality and spread of the model residuals.
qqnorm(residuals(fit))
qqline(residuals(fit))
plot(fitted(fit), residuals(fit),
     xlab = "Fitted group mean", ylab = "Residual")
abline(h = 0, col = "gray40")
tapply(dat$response, dat$group, sd)
</program>
    <p>Read the factor row in <c>summary(fit)</c>. Its <c>F value</c> is the test statistic and its <c>Pr(&gt;F)</c> entry is the p-value. A normal Q-Q plot should be reasonably straight; a residual plot should not show large changes in vertical spread across fitted group means. Independence comes from the study design and cannot be established by these plots.</p>
    <p>For every ANOVA example, identify the factor, response, and population means; state the hypotheses; assess the requirements using the information available; read or complete the table; and state the decision and conclusion about the claim. Unless another level is given, use <m>\alpha=0.05</m>.</p>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="av-examples">
    <title>ANOVA Examples</title>
    <example xml:id="av-summary-example">
      <title>Growth Media: A Partially Completed Table</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Independent cultures are randomly assigned to one of three growth media, with 10 cultures per medium. The response is biomass after a fixed time, in grams. A researcher claims that all three population mean biomasses are equal. Assume the ANOVA requirements are reasonable. The following summaries and partial ANOVA table are supplied; use the displayed relationships to fill the blanks.</p>
        <table xml:id="av-summary-data">
          <title>Supplied Group Summaries</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
              <cell>Medium</cell>
              <cell>n</cell>
              <cell>Mean biomass</cell>
              <cell>Sample SD</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>A</cell>
              <cell>10</cell>
              <cell>12.0</cell>
              <cell>2.0</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>B</cell>
              <cell>10</cell>
              <cell>14.0</cell>
              <cell>2.0</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>C</cell>
              <cell>10</cell>
              <cell>16.0</cell>
              <cell>2.0</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <table xml:id="av-partial-table">
          <title>Complete Only the Blank Entries</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
              <cell>Source</cell>
              <cell>df</cell>
              <cell>SS</cell>
              <cell>MS</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Medium</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>80</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Residuals</cell>
              <cell>27</cell>
              <cell>108</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Total</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>Use the completed table to find the F-statistic and its two degrees of freedom.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Use <c>pf()</c> to find the p-value and decide whether there is sufficient evidence to reject the equal-means claim.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Can this ANOVA alone establish that Medium C has a larger population mean than Medium B? Explain.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="av-raw-example">
      <title>Seedling Growth: Raw Data and aov()</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Twenty-four seedlings are randomly assigned to Control, Nutrient A, or Nutrient B, with eight seedlings per group. Each seedling is grown independently under otherwise comparable conditions. The response is final height in centimeters. A biologist claims that at least one population mean differs. Inspect the groups, fit the model, check residuals, and test the claim at <m>\alpha=0.05</m>. Save the fitted model for the Tukey comparisons.</p>
        <program language="r">
plants &lt;- data.frame(
  group = factor(rep(c("Control", "NutrientA", "NutrientB"),
                     each = 8),
                 levels = c("Control", "NutrientA", "NutrientB")),
  height = c(18, 22, 20, 25, 19, 23, 21, 20,
             24, 28, 26, 31, 25, 29, 27, 26,
             25, 29, 27, 32, 26, 30, 28, 27)
)
plant_fit &lt;- aov(height ~ group, data = plants)
summary(plant_fit)
</program>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="av-output-example">
      <title>Enzyme Activity: Interpreting Output</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Twenty-four independent cultures are randomly assigned to three preparation methods, eight per method. The response is enzyme activity in units per liter. A researcher claims that at least one population mean differs. Assume diagnostics show no material violation of the ANOVA requirements. A condensed ANOVA output table is shown below.</p>
        <table xml:id="av-enzyme-output">
          <title>ANOVA Output for Enzyme Activity</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
              <cell>Source</cell>
              <cell>Df</cell>
              <cell>Sum Sq</cell>
              <cell>Mean Sq</cell>
              <cell>F value</cell>
              <cell>Pr(&gt;F)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Method</cell>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>4.75</cell>
              <cell>2.375</cell>
              <cell>0.5816</cell>
              <cell>0.5677</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Residuals</cell>
              <cell>21</cell>
              <cell>85.75</cell>
              <cell>4.083</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>Identify the numerator and denominator degrees of freedom and the test statistic.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>At <m>\alpha=0.05</m>, do the data support the researcher’s claim?</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Does your decision establish that all three population means are equal? Explain.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Is a follow-up search for significant pairs called for under the course workflow of following a significant overall ANOVA with Tukey comparisons? Explain.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="av-tukey">
    <title>Tukey Comparisons</title>
    <definition xml:id="av-tukey-definition">
      <title>Tukey Pairwise Comparisons</title>
      <statement>
        <p><term>Tukey comparisons</term> compare each pair of group population means while accounting for the collection of comparisons. After a significant overall ANOVA in these notes, use Tukey’s method to identify the pairs for which there is evidence of a difference.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>
    <program language="r">
TukeyHSD(fit, "group", conf.level = 0.95)
</program>
    <table xml:id="av-tukey-columns">
      <title>Reading TukeyHSD Output</title>
      <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Column</cell>
          <cell>Meaning</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <c>diff</c>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <line>Estimated difference: mean of the first</line>
            <line>named group minus mean of the second.</line>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><c>lwr</c> and <c>upr</c></cell>
          <cell>
            <line>Endpoints of the simultaneous interval</line>
            <line>for that difference.</line>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <c>p adj</c>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <line>p-value adjusted for the set</line>
            <line>of pairwise comparisons.</line>
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>At a 5% significance level, a pair differs significantly when its adjusted p-value is below 0.05; equivalently, its 95% simultaneous interval excludes 0. Use the sign of <c>diff</c> to describe the direction. An interval containing 0 gives insufficient evidence of a difference; it does not prove equality. Repeated unadjusted t-tests do not provide the same control over the collection of comparisons.</p>
    <p>For a row labeled <c>B-A</c>, a positive difference estimates a larger population mean for B. The factor’s level order determines the row labels; always read the subtraction order in the row name.</p>
    <example xml:id="av-tukey-summary">
      <title>Growth Media: Supplied Pairwise Output</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Return to the growth-media summary-statistics example. The table below gives Tukey comparisons based on its supplied ANOVA information. Use <m>\alpha=0.05</m>.</p>
        <table xml:id="av-media-tukey">
          <title>Tukey Comparisons for Growth Media</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
              <cell>Pair</cell>
              <cell>diff</cell>
              <cell>lwr</cell>
              <cell>upr</cell>
              <cell>p adj</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>B-A</cell>
              <cell>2.000</cell>
              <cell>-0.218</cell>
              <cell>4.218</cell>
              <cell>0.0831</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>C-A</cell>
              <cell>4.000</cell>
              <cell>1.782</cell>
              <cell>6.218</cell>
              <cell>0.0004</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>C-B</cell>
              <cell>2.000</cell>
              <cell>-0.218</cell>
              <cell>4.218</cell>
              <cell>0.0831</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>Identify the pairs with evidence of different population means.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Interpret the sign and endpoints of the C-A comparison in grams.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Explain how a significant overall ANOVA can occur even when some pairwise comparisons are not significant.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="av-tukey-raw">
      <title>Seedlings: Generate and Interpret Tukey Output</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Use the raw seedling data and the saved <c>plant_fit</c> model. Run the command below, identify the significant pairs, and explain the direction of each supported difference. Then explain why the observed sample mean ranking does not automatically establish every population mean ranking.</p>
        <program language="r">
TukeyHSD(plant_fit, "group", conf.level = 0.95)
</program>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="av-large-data">
    <title>A Larger Health Data Set</title>
    <example xml:id="av-large-study">
      <title>Large Data Example: Comparing Three Programs</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The supplied file <c>data/health-study-360.csv</c> contains simulated data for 360 adults. In the hypothetical design, a simple random sample is selected from a large population of eligible adults, and participants are randomly assigned to Control, Walking, or Combined programs, with 120 in each group. Control receives usual information, Walking adds a walking program, and Combined adds walking plus nutrition counseling. Systolic blood pressure is recorded before assignment and after eight weeks. Each row is one participant; both measurements are in millimeters of mercury. The simulation is for statistical practice and does not establish clinical effects.</p>
        <program language="r">
health &lt;- read.csv("data/health-study-360.csv")
health$group &lt;- factor(health$group,
                       levels = c("Control", "Walking", "Combined"))
health$reduction &lt;- health$baseline_sbp - health$week8_sbp
</program>
        <p>Use one reduction per participant, with positive values indicating a decrease in blood pressure. A researcher claims that at least one program has a different population mean reduction. Use <m>\alpha=0.05</m>.</p>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>Identify the response, factor, levels, and experimental unit. State the null and alternative hypotheses and identify where the claim belongs.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Find the sample size, mean reduction, and standard deviation for each group. Create side-by-side boxplots.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Fit the one-way ANOVA model below. Inspect a normal Q-Q plot of the residuals and a plot of residuals against fitted values. Comment on normality, outliers, and similarity of spreads.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Use the output to complete the partial table. Find the p-value and state the conclusion about the claim.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>If the overall ANOVA is significant, run <c>TukeyHSD(health_fit, "group")</c>. Identify which programs differ and report the direction and estimated size of each supported difference in mmHg.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>For the Combined-Walking comparison, explain what the adjusted p-value says and interpret the simultaneous interval. Explain why the Tukey adjusted p-value need not equal the Welch p-value from the two-sample notes.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Explain why repeated baseline and week-eight values were reduced to one change per person for this ANOVA. Discuss the scope of the conclusions from the hypothetical sampling and random assignment.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <program language="r">
tapply(health$reduction, health$group, length)
tapply(health$reduction, health$group, mean)
tapply(health$reduction, health$group, sd)
boxplot(reduction ~ group, data = health,
        ylab = "Systolic blood pressure reduction (mmHg)")
health_fit &lt;- aov(reduction ~ group, data = health)
summary(health_fit)

qqnorm(residuals(health_fit))
qqline(residuals(health_fit))
plot(fitted(health_fit), residuals(health_fit),
     xlab = "Fitted mean reduction", ylab = "Residual")
abline(h = 0, col = "gray40")
</program>
        <table xml:id="av-health-partial">
          <title>Complete the Health-Study ANOVA Table</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
              <cell>Source</cell>
              <cell>df</cell>
              <cell>SS</cell>
              <cell>MS</cell>
              <cell>F</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Program</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>1766.1095</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Residuals</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>11664.21025</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Total</cell>
              <cell>359</cell>
              <cell>___</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <!-- Sources checked: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-patched/library/stats/html/aov.html ; https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-patched/library/stats/html/TukeyHSD.html -->
</section>
